From 8ef834a63cd210f943fc033682f688e3a7b975f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Corios00 <52224974+Corios00@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:32:36 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] The client asks for the fast GPU outright and writes a perf
 log every ten seconds

Corey's laptop has an Intel integrated GPU and an RTX 5070 Laptop, and Elwynn ran badly there with
no log to say which one drew it or where the frame went.

- The GPU: `tuned_default_plugins` sets `WgpuSettings::power_preference` to `HighPerformance`
  (still `WGPU_POWER_PREF` first), which is bevy's default today but now said outright: under it
  wgpu ranks a discrete adapter before an integrated one. On Windows the binary also exports
  `NvOptimusEnablement` and `AmdPowerXpressRequestHighPerformance` (`build.rs` passes MSVC's
  linker `/EXPORT`, since an executable exports nothing on its own), the hints NVIDIA's and AMD's
  laptop drivers read to put a process on the discrete GPU.
- The log: `benilla-config/perf.log`, always on (`perf::perf_log`). At start, the adapter in use
  (name, backend, device type, vendor, driver), with a warning on an integrated or software one;
  whenever they change, the window, present mode, refresh rate, render scale, MSAA, farclip and
  the look's reach, shadows, fog and AO; every ten seconds the zone and subzone, the position, the
  fps, the wall frame time's p50, p95, p99 and max, the frames over 33 and 100 ms, the CPU per
  frame (main thread and process), the GPU per frame and its six costliest passes (bevy's per-pass
  timestamps), the meshes drawn after the frustum cull and the entity count. Per frame it pushes
  one float. Past 2 MB the file moves to `perf.old.log` at the next start.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
